  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 1129 PM MST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nA mix of stratus, mist, and fog are evident across the eastern\nplains tonight. Based on surface observations, most of the lower\nvisibilities seem to be east of the main stratus deck that has\npersisted across Lincoln county into the far northeastern corner\nthis evening. Short term guidance continues to develop additional\nfog across most of the Platte River valley into the\nnorthern/northwestern Denver metro, though recent HRRR runs have\nbacked off of the southwestern extent of this fog through Monday\nAM. We've kept patchy fog in the grids across most of the lower\nelevations but did trim some of it out of the I-25 corridor. \n\nMonday should be a mild day across most of our forecast area as\nthe ridge axis shifts eastward. We'll have broad but dry\nsouthwesterly flow aloft during the day ahead of our next\nshortwave, with temperatures again reaching the low to mid 60s\nacross the plains. Similar conditions are expected on Tuesday as\nthe shortwave continues to approach the region and the mid-level\nflow strengthens in response. Highs are likely to reach the mid\n60s in the metro with some guidance hinting at potentially warmer\ntemperatures.\n\nThe first of two storm systems will reach the area on Wednesday,\nthough this one looks to only provide a glancing blow to the\nregion as it quickly ejects into the northern Rockies/northern\nGreat Plains. Guidance does have a decent push of moisture across\nthe western half of the state Tuesday night, but quickly dries us\nout as the mid-level low pushes into northeastern Montana during \nthe day Wednesday. While the 700-500mb flow briefly is favorable \nfor orographic snow showers, the limited duration of sufficient \nmoisture will keep snow accumulations to a minimum (generally an \ninch or less). The main story this period may be wind, as this \nlooks to be our first bora type event of the season. Cross \nsections reveal a brief/marginally favorable mountain wave \namplification setup Wednesday morning. I am a bit skeptical of \nthe higher end solution advertised by the RRFS at this time, but \n50kt cross barrier flow along with a modest stable layer would \nsupport a few gusts near high wind criteria over the Front Range \nmountains and higher foothills. On the other hand, any additional \nnorthward shift with this shortwave would likely limit wind \npotential across our forecast area.\n\nThere should be a brief break between systems on Thursday, with\nmild and generally quiet weather across the state. The next system\narrives Friday and looks a little more organized and longer \nlasting, as a broad trough axis moves through the region. While \nthis is still unlikely to produce much (other than maybe some \ngusty winds) across the plains, the overall setup does look more \nfavorable for mountain snow across the northern half of the state.\nGuidance generally features a longer period of orographic snow, \ninitially supported by some QG ascent and a favorable jet \nposition. Chances for snow should linger into Saturday before\nmoisture diminishes on Sunday.",
